---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/docs/Callout.astro';
import { page } from '../../data/docs-nav';
import { GITHUB_URL, DONATE_URL, LICENSE_URL } from '../../data/site';

const entry = page('/docs/free-and-open/');
---

<DocsLayout page={entry} section="Start here">
  <p>
    Most apps in this category have a free tier that cannot do the thing you installed them for, and
    a paid tier that can. Hozz does not have tiers. Every feature described anywhere in this
    documentation is in the app, and there is nothing to buy.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is worth stating plainly rather than implying, because if you are comparing exporters it is
    the difference that decides most of the others.
  </p>

  <h2>What there is none of</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>No account.</strong> Not an optional one, not a free one — there is nothing to sign in
      to, because there is no service behind it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No subscription and no paid tier.</strong> No feature is withheld, time-limited, or
      capped by record count.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No server run by the maintainer.</strong> Your data never passes through
      infrastructure belonging to whoever wrote this. There is no relay, no queue, and no "cloud" in
      the middle.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No analytics, telemetry, or crash reporting.</strong> Diagnostics stay on the device,
      and redact sample values, credentials, and destination secrets.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No advertising, and no data sold.</strong> There is no mechanism by which it could be:
      the data does not arrive anywhere it could be sold from.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>No default destination.</strong> Nothing is sent anywhere until you add a destination
      and confirm it, because a default would be a place someone else chose.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How that is possible</h2>

  <p>
    Because there is no server, there is almost nothing to pay for. An exporter that uploads your
    Health data to its own backend has real hosting costs, real storage costs, and a real reason to
    need revenue from you. Hozz moves data from your phone to a destination you already own and
    already pay for — your Mac, your iCloud Drive, your Home Assistant, your InfluxDB. The expensive
    part of the usual architecture is the part Hozz does not have.
  </p>

  <p>
    The remaining cost is one developer's time, which is donated. If you want to support that,{' '}
    <a href={DONATE_URL}>GitHub Sponsors</a> exists, and it buys you nothing — there is no supporter
    tier, no badge and no unlocked feature, because a feature unlocked by paying is exactly the
    thing this page says does not exist.
  </p>

  <h2>What "open source" gets you here</h2>

  <p>
    Hozz is <a href={LICENSE_URL}>GPL-3.0 with an App Store distribution exception</a> and developed
    in the open. That matters more than usual for this kind of app, because the entire claim being
    made is a negative one — <em>we do not send your data anywhere you did not choose</em> — and a
    negative claim cannot be demonstrated by using the app. You can only check it by reading it.
  </p>

  <p>So the design tries to make it checkable rather than trustworthy-sounding:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href={GITHUB_URL}>Every line is readable</a>, including the networking and the parts that
      touch the Keychain.
    </li>
    <li>
      Privacy behaviours have tests, rather than being assertions on a page. There are 520 tests in
      the suite, and some of them exist specifically to fail if a Health value ever reached a log.
    </li>
    <li>
      This documentation is written against that source, and the lists in it are{' '}
      <a href={`${GITHUB_URL}`}>checked against the app's own enums</a> mechanically rather than copied
      by hand.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <Callout kind="note" title="What is honestly not free">
    <p>
      Hozz is not in the App Store yet, so today you install it by building it, which needs a Mac
      and Xcode. That is a real cost in effort even though it is not a cost in money, and it is the
      main reason to choose something else for now.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <h2>The one thing that can still leak</h2>

  <p>
    Hozz does not send your data anywhere you did not configure. It cannot make any promise about
    what happens after that.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you point Hozz at an endpoint on the public internet, the data is on that endpoint. If you
    connect the <a href="/docs/mcp/">MCP server</a> to a cloud-hosted assistant, that assistant may
    upload whatever it reads — that is the assistant's behaviour, and no local server can prevent
    it. Choosing destinations you actually control is the whole discipline the app is built around,
    and it is the one part it cannot do for you.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/docs/privacy/">Privacy</a> sets out exactly what leaves the device, when, and to where.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
